On 2010-08-01, Rob Jarratt <nospam@nospam.no> wrote:
> I did a web search for this error and some suggestions about the old block 
> size thing came up (512 instead of the larger block size used nowadays). Is 
> the block size going to be something in the ISO file I am trying to burn, or 
> in the burner I am using perhaps?

No, it would just be in the drive reading the disk. CDs are organized
around 2048 byte blocks. The 512 capable drives allow each 2048-byte
block to be addressed and read as four 512-byte blocks.

I have an old DVD player that cannot read media that is faster than about
4x or so. Makes no difference how slowly I burn the disk, the faster
media use a different dye that the player can't see; it probably doesn't
provide enough contrast. I suspect that's the sort of thing going on here.
-- 
roger ivie
rivie@ridgenet.net


